<.shell title="Overview" current={:index}>
  <h1>Style guide</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A design system for operational tools: search and rescue, emergency services, and teams
    like them. GOV.UK's patterns and plain look, Carbon's dense tables, in light and dark.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Plain and calm.</strong>
      People use this between calls and after long days. Short words, one primary action per page,
      nothing that moves without a reason.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dense where people scan, roomy where they type.</strong>
      Tables use 32px rows, or 24px when compact. Forms keep 40px inputs and big targets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Desktop first, phone second.</strong>
      Pages are designed for a laptop. On a phone everything still works and nothing breaks:
      layouts stack, and tables scroll sideways with the useful columns first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyboard first.</strong>
      Everything you can reach shows the yellow focus, the same in both modes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fixed scales.</strong>
      Six type sizes, eight spaces, and a named palette. No value is invented on a page; if a
      design needs a new one, it joins the scale.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tokens, then modes.</strong>
      Components use colour tokens only, so dark mode is a second set of values, not a second
      set of styles.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pages</h2>
  <dl class="summary">
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:colors)}>Colours</a></dt>
      <dd>Every token in light and dark, and what each is for.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:typography)}>Typography</a></dt>
      <dd>The type scale, headings, body text, numbers, and links.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:writing)}>Writing</a></dt>
      <dd>The rules and glossary for every word a person reads. Agents follow it.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:icons)}>Icons</a></dt>
      <dd>The small set of icons, their sizes, and when to use one.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:layout)}>Layout</a></dt>
      <dd>Spacing, page headers, summary lists, panels, and stats.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:components)}>Buttons and tags</a></dt>
      <dd>Button kinds and sizes, tags, and focus.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:navigation)}>Navigation</a></dt>
      <dd>Breadcrumbs, back links, tabs, and pagination.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:feedback)}>Messages</a></dt>
      <dd>Toasts, banners, warnings, errors, empty states, and loading.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:tables)}>Tables</a></dt>
      <dd>Dense and compact tables, from the app's real pages.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href={page_path(:forms)}>Forms</a></dt>
      <dd>Every input, errors, and the app's real forms.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</.shell>
